WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.720
So, we are all set up. As you mentioned as

2
00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:06.880
well, we have seated next to our pictures.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:10.040
So, in case there are any misunderstandings,

4
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.440
you know who is speaking on the panel.

5
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.040
So, thank you everybody for staying with us for the continuation

6
00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:17.940
of our sessions.

7
00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:25.120
Even though my colleague didn't manage to mention one of the

8
00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:31.640
key happenings in the news over this night that would also set

9
00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:34.760
the scene for us. So, perhaps some of you already know that last

10
00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:39.240
night around 3:00 a.m. two ships in the Bulgarian exclusive economic

11
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:42.880
zone were under a drone attack.

12
00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.910
One of the ships has sunk and thankfully all of the

13
00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.720
mariners are safe. They're already back here in Varna.

14
00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:59.760
But this attack happened 80 km from the shore of Byala,

15
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.750
which is a bit south of Varna.

16
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:08.720
This is actually the first attack on vessels

17
00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:10.430
in the Bulgarian economic zone.

18
00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.200
And right now, I think that exactly at 11:00 the prime minister is

19
00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:18.880
going to give a press conference on the topic and we are very eager

20
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:20.630
to hear more details

21
00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:24.630
about the following

22
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:31.640
investigation. The threats are here.

23
00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:35.880
They're happening. It's quite a coincidence that it's happening

24
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.430
during our conference.

25
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:43.430
So, having that in mind, I will first start with Professor Veenstra.

26
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.680
And with the question. So, when there is such an occasion,

27
00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:52.070
such a disruption,

28
00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:59.000
and the system that is coordinating the movement of the

29
00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.550
vessels is being either compromised or unavailable,

30
00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:06.320
where does this disruption spread fastest?

31
00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:10.119
So, in which link of the systems?

32
00:02:10.119 --> 00:02:14.280
And in your opinion, which fallback arrangements should those

33
00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:19.240
organizations that are part of the supply chain have tested

34
00:02:19.240 --> 00:02:22.790
before an incident of this scale occurs?

35
00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:28.080
Yeah. It's an interesting question because it gives me the

36
00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:30.800
opportunity to talk a little bit about some of the research we've

37
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:35.200
been doing in the Rotterdam area for the last three

38
00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.030
years or so. So,

39
00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:43.830
in Rotterdam we have been concerned about cybersecurity,

40
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:47.950
cyber attacks on parties in the port communities

41
00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:52.000
and the business ecosystem,

42
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:55.000
you could say, for quite some time.

43
00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:59.440
But nobody... Well, there was also a European regulation coming in,

44
00:02:59.440 --> 00:03:04.680
NIS2, so a few years ago a lot of the business community started to

45
00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.720
become really nervous and say, "Okay, this regulation is coming.

46
00:03:07.720 --> 00:03:10.120
We have this significant threat.

47
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.480
What do we need to do?"

48
00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:15.519
So, we created a project that went around to talk to various

49
00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:18.480
parties in this business ecosystem and we're talking about port

50
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:23.560
operations, so ship cargo handling, but also port services.

51
00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:27.040
We have business services and insurance and ship broking and a

52
00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:29.470
lot of the other business-related activities.

53
00:03:29.959 --> 00:03:31.959
Also shipbuilding and ship repair.

54
00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:35.120
So, the business ecosystem around the port of Rotterdam is a

55
00:03:35.120 --> 00:03:38.360
really rich ecosystem with lots of different activities.

56
00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:41.280
And we also have a big industrial cluster with chemical companies

57
00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.080
and refineries and all that.

58
00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.080
All of them have their own cyber challenges.

59
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:48.840
All of them look at it in their own way.

60
00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.390
And we try to understand a little bit about vulnerabilities.

61
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:55.320
Exactly as you sketched: what if an attack happens,

62
00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:58.360
is it then an attack on a single company and does it remain

63
00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:02.390
contained to the single company or will it spread in a certain way?

64
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:05.480
This is where we found out, as I said in the previous session,

65
00:04:05.480 --> 00:04:06.870
that ships are relatively

66
00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.880
not so vulnerable because they are not really connected to

67
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:15.280
the business ecosystem. People communicate with the

68
00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.760
vessels through VHF or through email or something,

69
00:04:18.760 --> 00:04:23.320
but not in a connected way for the majority of the ships anyway.

70
00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:26.000
The business ecosystem on land, on the other hand,

71
00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.120
is quite connected. There is a port community system,

72
00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:32.080
Portbase, that is facilitating a lot of the data

73
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:36.000
interaction. There is then the port operational system,

74
00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:39.800
HARMS, it's called, that facilitates, let's say, all the

75
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:43.280
operational support for the harbor master to deal with the ships,

76
00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:46.320
so the planning, the VTS integration,

77
00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:50.600
but also all the commercial parts of that, the invoicing for port

78
00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:52.590
dues and stuff like that, it's all in HARMS.

79
00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:56.480
So these two systems are the backbone of the entire

80
00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.230
port operation for a large part of the ecosystem.

81
00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:02.919
And as a result, these are vulnerable,

82
00:05:02.919 --> 00:05:06.910
but at the same time, because we're not entirely stupid,

83
00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:10.120
these systems are also designed in a very robust way.

84
00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.640
So we have a company called Schuberg Philis,

85
00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:17.120
which is a company that specializes in cyber-robust data

86
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:18.630
infrastructure, IT infrastructure.

87
00:05:19.160 --> 00:05:22.960
They are rebuilding the HARMS system at the moment, including the

88
00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.120
radar system, so the Port of Rotterdam, to be completely cyber

89
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:32.520
robust. So we have this backbone, but the backbone is,

90
00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:35.990
I think I can confidently say, a very robust IT infrastructure.

91
00:05:36.800 --> 00:05:39.160
That is not the case for all the other companies that are around

92
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:42.960
this system. So a lot of the agents,

93
00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:47.440
a lot of the other parties, some of them do some work on

94
00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.230
cybersecurity, some of them don't.

95
00:05:49.720 --> 00:05:54.840
We've had some quite high-profile hacks in the Port of

96
00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.760
Rotterdam in the last few years.

97
00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:00.080
And the most famous of those is the APM Terminals hack,

98
00:06:00.080 --> 00:06:04.480
which was a global problem that also played out in Rotterdam.

99
00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.550
But more recently, we have a tiny company called Dirkzwager,

100
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.160
which is a company nobody has ever heard of if you're not from

101
00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:15.950
Rotterdam. But it's a company that deals with some really crucial

102
00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:21.240
services. For instance, the visit register for anybody who

103
00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.560
wants to visit the ship that is at a quay, that system is managed by

104
00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:30.000
Dirkzwager. So, if their system is out, there's no possibility to

105
00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:33.040
visit ships anymore. So, that means no family,

106
00:06:33.040 --> 00:06:36.600
but also no supplies, no doctor, no crew change,

107
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:41.160
nothing can happen for a while until that system or a backup of it

108
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.070
is back online. And that hack happened in 2023.

109
00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:49.360
And then we found out that the port ecosystem is a funny ecosystem

110
00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:52.040
in a sense that you have parties that don't exist anywhere else in

111
00:06:52.040 --> 00:06:53.550
the economy. And we have pilots.

112
00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:57.400
Nobody knows what pilots are if you're not from a port environment.

113
00:06:57.400 --> 00:06:59.110
We have people called boatmen.

114
00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:03.200
These are guys that are moving around when a boat needs to moor,

115
00:07:03.200 --> 00:07:05.270
they pick up the rope and tie it to the quay.

116
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.160
This doesn't exist anywhere else.

117
00:07:08.160 --> 00:07:11.240
We have these companies like Dirkzwager who provide these really

118
00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:15.310
peculiar services that don't exist anywhere else.

119
00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:19.040
So, the port ecosystem at the same time, I think the backbone of it

120
00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:22.960
is fairly robust, but at the same time, a lot of the environment

121
00:07:22.960 --> 00:07:27.680
of it, the different parties and systems, are quite

122
00:07:27.680 --> 00:07:29.560
vulnerable, some of them.

123
00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:34.840
And another issue that plays out is the dependency in

124
00:07:34.840 --> 00:07:37.520
this network, because none of these parties operate on

125
00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:41.400
their own. To bring in a ship, you need a cooperation of

126
00:07:41.400 --> 00:07:45.720
pilots, tugboats, boatmen, agents, customs,

127
00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:48.990
customs brokers, terminals, the port authority,

128
00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.520
the harbor master. There's a whole bunch of parties that then

129
00:07:52.520 --> 00:07:56.160
suddenly get involved. All of them need to communicate somehow at

130
00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.150
some point. And if this communication is destroyed or obstructed,

131
00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:03.840
then that operation cannot continue.

132
00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:08.680
So the system has a large degree of dependency,

133
00:08:08.680 --> 00:08:11.520
and we are only beginning, I think, to understand a little bit what

134
00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:15.880
this dependency means, both in terms of the operational side of it,

135
00:08:15.880 --> 00:08:17.910
but also in terms of the IT.

136
00:08:18.640 --> 00:08:24.040
So, I think it's not for nothing that in Rotterdam we have

137
00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:27.960
for about a decade a system to create cyber

138
00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.430
resilience. It's called FERM.

139
00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:33.240
F-E-R-M. Yeah, that's it.

140
00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:35.310
FERM. And FERM has

141
00:08:36.919 --> 00:08:40.560
made a sort of a transition in the last few years where they are

142
00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:44.880
really starting to be... Their focus was usually on the port

143
00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:48.400
business ecosystem, but they have moved back to

144
00:08:48.400 --> 00:08:51.880
focus on the port. So, they are now the cyber resilience initiative

145
00:08:51.880 --> 00:08:54.440
in the Netherlands for the five seaports that we have: Rotterdam,

146
00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:58.280
Amsterdam, Groningen, North Sea Ports, which is in the south,

147
00:08:58.280 --> 00:09:04.280
and Moerdijk. And they are more concerned about, let's

148
00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:08.080
say, the continued operation of the port in relation to cyber

149
00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:13.120
attacks or in relation to societal disturbance of any kind,

150
00:09:13.120 --> 00:09:18.400
I guess. So they have really focused a lot on what would

151
00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:20.680
have to happen for the port to stop operating.

152
00:09:20.680 --> 00:09:23.480
And that means that on the one hand you can discard a lot of issues.

153
00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:26.670
Say, a single company that has a ransomware attack,

154
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:30.160
yeah, I don't need to be too concerned about that because it will

155
00:09:30.160 --> 00:09:32.200
take this company out of business for a while,

156
00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:37.600
but mostly they're quickly restored and the port will keep

157
00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:41.560
operating. So they have been looking at cyber hacks

158
00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:44.280
focused on the IT infrastructure, focused on the internet,

159
00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:48.440
focused on electricity, focused on... and we have this water

160
00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:49.920
barrier in the port of Rotterdam.

161
00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:53.800
If somebody takes that over and closes it at a certain

162
00:09:53.800 --> 00:09:56.440
point so that ships will bump into it, then the port will be out of

163
00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.360
business for quite some time.

164
00:09:58.360 --> 00:10:00.510
They focus on these kinds of attacks.

165
00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.240
And that helps, I think, focus the attention of

166
00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:07.480
port authorities, but also of the national government.

167
00:10:07.480 --> 00:10:10.360
They want a big stake in how we organize our cyber resilience in

168
00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:12.270
the Netherlands at the moment.

169
00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:17.640
It also leaves things unsolved, and I think one of the biggest

170
00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:21.560
issues that we have not really tackled yet is if there is a

171
00:10:21.560 --> 00:10:25.440
high-scale attack on the port of Rotterdam, and we already know

172
00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:29.760
the port of Rotterdam is on a hit list of Putin because he published

173
00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:33.870
a sort of a hit list and Rotterdam is on there somewhere.

174
00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:38.480
So, we know we are in the crosshairs already.

175
00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:40.870
So, if there is a large-scale attack,

176
00:10:42.800 --> 00:10:46.640
the defense, how you organize the collective defense against this,

177
00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:49.280
this is a big challenge still, I think.

178
00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:51.920
And the people in FERM, the port authority,

179
00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.760
a lot of other people are thinking about how we should be doing

180
00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:58.760
this. And they are running into issues that Shell and BP and

181
00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.480
some of the other large companies say, "Oh, yeah,

182
00:11:00.480 --> 00:11:02.360
I have my defense organized already.

183
00:11:02.360 --> 00:11:05.800
It's no problem. If somebody attacks me, I will find a way."

184
00:11:05.800 --> 00:11:09.040
Or I'll close everything off and I will deal with it.

185
00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:11.560
So, yeah, but you are part of an industrial cluster,

186
00:11:11.560 --> 00:11:14.760
so what if the attack proliferates through you to somebody else?

187
00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:17.080
Yeah, well, that's somebody else's problem then.

188
00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:19.750
There's another company that needs to deal with that separately.

189
00:11:20.400 --> 00:11:25.920
But a collective defense where a collective

190
00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.720
counterattack or protection effort could be organized, that is

191
00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:33.160
something that is extremely difficult to come up with, to

192
00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:37.430
conceptualize and also to organize in the current state of affairs.

193
00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:42.040
And the government, our government, has a role in this, the NCTV and

194
00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:46.040
the NCSC, which is the cyber security monitoring organization

195
00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:47.550
that we put together.

196
00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:51.880
Then they say, yeah, it's all fine as long as you are thinking about it

197
00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:54.600
and you will inform us when something happens, then

198
00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:58.360
we're happy. So in the port the people who are concerned about

199
00:11:58.360 --> 00:11:59.960
this then say, yeah, inform you.

200
00:11:59.960 --> 00:12:02.270
Why would I inform you? Are you going to help me with the defense?

201
00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:05.920
No, no, you just need to tell us what's going on so that we can report

202
00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:07.990
it properly according to European law.

203
00:12:08.480 --> 00:12:12.480
Yeah, okay, but don't waste my time with these kinds of ideas.

204
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:15.880
I need help when I really have to fight back against attackers and

205
00:12:15.880 --> 00:12:19.760
figure out what's going on. So this debate is currently going on on how

206
00:12:19.760 --> 00:12:23.470
to organize this and I think this is one of the major issues

207
00:12:24.040 --> 00:12:25.760
that we are currently struggling with.

208
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:27.280
So I think the awareness is fine.

209
00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:30.680
A lot of companies have sorted out their robust infrastructure

210
00:12:30.680 --> 00:12:34.360
already. Companies like Schuberg Philis are making a crazy amount of money

211
00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:37.550
in rebuilding some of this infrastructure to make it more robust.

212
00:12:38.000 --> 00:12:41.800
But the real collective... if you see cyber attack as a collective

213
00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:45.000
problem, the real collective solution for that is still a little bit

214
00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:47.640
outside our grasp, I think.

215
00:12:47.640 --> 00:12:51.590
And so that becomes a problem as soon as it

216
00:12:52.240 --> 00:12:55.920
paralyzes the port. So not all these problems are really problems of

217
00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:00.280
that magnitude. In this project we have

218
00:13:00.280 --> 00:13:03.440
identified quite a lot of the business community that you

219
00:13:03.440 --> 00:13:07.960
can ignore because these are single-person companies that work in

220
00:13:07.960 --> 00:13:12.110
Microsoft 365, and if they're hacked, not really a problem.

221
00:13:12.600 --> 00:13:16.520
But conceptualize an attack that will paralyze the port

222
00:13:16.520 --> 00:13:20.080
and then figure out how to fight back, that's still, I think, on

223
00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:21.670
the agenda. Yeah.

224
00:13:22.080 --> 00:13:27.560
Thank you so much, Professor, for laying out the framework of

225
00:13:27.560 --> 00:13:32.640
the topic, and you have mentioned several times the

226
00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:37.400
role of IT and the communications perspective.

227
00:13:37.400 --> 00:13:39.280
So, I would now turn to Mr.

228
00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:44.400
Zimbilev, and I would also like to know from your perspective,

229
00:13:44.400 --> 00:13:47.070
coming from, you know, the cloud service providers,

230
00:13:47.800 --> 00:13:52.680
which are the dependencies that infrastructures like ports and

231
00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:57.520
the shipping companies in them might overlook when

232
00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.800
they're preparing for a cyber incident.

233
00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:04.110
How can we avoid, from your perspective, such incidents?

234
00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:10.680
Thank you. Before I dive into the question, just for the audience

235
00:14:10.680 --> 00:14:16.520
context, to share where I'm coming from, CloudOffice today is one

236
00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:20.200
of the two authorized managed security service providers of Google

237
00:14:20.200 --> 00:14:24.480
SecOps Suite. We're headquartered here in Bulgaria with

238
00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:28.560
facilities in this region, Cyprus, Athens, and the United Kingdom,

239
00:14:28.560 --> 00:14:33.830
and serving about 850 customers across approximately 40 countries.

240
00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.030
With that being said, cybersecurity

241
00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:42.960
is a topic that, you know, everybody is very interested in,

242
00:14:42.960 --> 00:14:44.950
especially during forums

243
00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:49.830
and broader discussions, but in the real business

